HELP(1)                       Funny CLI Manual                       HELP(1)

NAME
    help, ? — Display available commands, because remembering what
              you wrote yesterday is apparently unreasonable.

SYNOPSIS
    help
    ?

DESCRIPTION
    The 'help' command displays a list of all commands currently
    available in the CLI.

    For each command, it prints the command's help text when one exists.
    If a command has aliases, those are displayed underneath it as well.

    In short, this command answers the timeless developer question:

        "Wait... what commands do I actually have?"

    No judgment. We've all been there.

ALIASES
    ?
        An alias for 'help'.

        Because even typing four letters to ask for help can feel
        like too much commitment.

OUTPUT
    The command begins with:

        Available commands:

    Each command is then listed with its help text.

    If aliases exist, they appear beneath the command:

        aliases: alias1, alias2

    Aliases are sorted alphabetically, because even chaos deserves
    a little organization.

EXAMPLES
    > help
        Available commands:
         - ls [-a/-l] [path]
           aliases: dir, la, ll
         - cd [path]
         - pwd
         - cat <file>
         - head <file> [-n lines]
         ...

        Suddenly the CLI has documentation.

    > ?
        Available commands:
         - ...

        Same information.

        Fewer keystrokes.

        More mysterious energy.

BEHAVIOR
    Commands with explicit help text display that text.

    Commands without help text display their command name instead.

    Aliases are discovered automatically and displayed underneath
    their corresponding commands.

    The command does not execute anything else.

    It merely tells you what you could be doing instead of asking
    questions.

FUN FACTS
    - 'help' documents the commands registered with the CLI.
    - '?' provides a tiny shortcut for requesting assistance.
    - Aliases are shown only when they actually exist.
    - Alias names are sorted alphabetically.
    - Commands without help text still appear, because mysterious
      commands deserve representation too.
    - Running 'help' is not a sign of weakness.
    - Running 'help' repeatedly instead of reading the source code
      is a perfectly valid development methodology.

SEE ALSO
    man(1), ls(1), cd(1), pwd(1), cat(1)

AUTHOR
    The Developer Who Forgot What They Named Their Own Commands